Assemble the 16-bit and 32-bit code a kernel starts in, and the system
instructions of its 64-bit code, as GNU as does.

An x86-64 kernel that a Multiboot loader starts in protected mode enters
long mode in 32-bit code of its own, and the other processors of a
machine start in 16-bit real mode, in a trampoline that goes on through
32-bit code to 64-bit code. GNU as takes .code16, .code32 and .code64
for such code. TinyCC 0.9.27's x86-64 assembler took none of the first
two, and its i386 assembler took .code16 and went on writing 32-bit code
under it. Both now write the code of the size the directive names, the
x86-64 one in any of the three and the i386 one in 16 or 32 bits, and a
file starts in its target's own:

  * An instruction whose operand is not of the code's size has the
    operand-size prefix 0x66, and one whose address registers are not of
    the code's address size the address-size prefix 0x67. In 16-bit code
    memory is addressed through %bx or %bp, %si or %di, or a 16-bit
    displacement, with the 16-bit ModRM forms, and a near jump or call
    has a 16-bit displacement.
  * The x86-64 assembler takes, in 16-bit and 32-bit code, what only that
    code has: push and pop of a 32-bit register, the one-byte inc and
    dec, a move between %eax and an absolute address, the moves of the
    control and debug registers through a 32-bit register, pusha and
    popa, and ljmp and lcall to a selector and an offset. It refuses
    there what needs a REX prefix or 64-bit code.
  * The suffixes GNU as gives an instruction whose operand is implicit or
    a pointer in memory: w, l or q on pushf, popf, iret, lret and ret, as
    the code size allows, lret and ret with the count of bytes they take
    off the stack or without it; w or l on pusha, popa, ljmp and lcall, and
    on lgdt, lidt, sgdt and sidt. lss, lfs and lgs take a 16-bit register.

The difference of two labels whose first comes later in the file, as
`lgdtl gdt_ptr - start + BASE` in a trampoline copied to BASE, became a
PC-relative relocation whose addend took the field to begin at the start
of the instruction, so every such value in an instruction came out short
by the length of the opcode and ModRM bytes ahead of its field, with no
diagnostic. The code that writes the field now adds the field's own
offset. A 16-bit or 64-bit field takes such a difference too, and a
16-bit one a symbol: R_X86_64_16 and R_X86_64_PC16, or R_386_16 and
R_386_PC16, which the linkers now apply in an ELF file, with a value
that does not fit an error, as with GNU ld.

The x86-64 assembler gains the 64-bit iretq and lretq, the 16-bit retw,
the XSAVE instructions (xsave, xrstor, xsaveopt and xsavec, and their
64-bit forms xsave64 and the others), fxsave64 and fxrstor64 beside
fxsaveq and fxrstorq, xgetbv, xsetbv and rdtscp. The i386 assembler
gains the same but the 64-bit forms.

Code of the target's own size is assembled as before, byte for byte: the
object of every source of the Rach2 kernel is unchanged on either target.
Neither assembler relaxes a forward jump: loop and jecxz to a label later
in the file are still an error, and jmp and jcc to one take the long form.

